  "nav.automation": "Automation",
  "nav.automation.desc": "Plug-ins & processes inventory",
  "automation.subtitle": "Everything registered to run in the org — plug-in steps, workflows, business rules, actions, BPFs, flows. The design-time inventory; System Ops shows the runtime jobs.",
  "help.automation_title": "Automation inventory",
  "help.automation_body": "The static inventory of the org's automation: plug-in STEP registrations (which class runs on which message/entity, Pre-validation / Pre-operation / Post-operation, sync or async, enabled or disabled, filtering attributes on hover) and every process definition from the workflow table — classic workflows (with Background/Real-time mode and Create/Update/Delete triggers), business rules, actions, BPFs, Power Automate cloud flows, dialogs and desktop flows. Filter by state, by SOURCE, or free text (name, entity, message, assembly); every view exports to CSV/Excel. Source is a best-effort call because Dataverse doesn't stamp who wrote a component: Microsoft = publisher-prefix heuristic (Microsoft.* assemblies, msdyn-family names — and plenty of Microsoft's own steps are registered UNMANAGED by the platform, so the managed flag alone would mislabel them); Managed = installed from a managed solution (an ISV's or your own); Custom = unmanaged. Internal steps (stage 30 MainOperation and friends — the platform's own execution machinery, which the Plugin Registration Tool also hides) are hidden by default behind a toggle. Unlike System Ops (which shows runtime JOBS), this shows what is REGISTERED to fire — the map you want before debugging \"why did this field change?\" or before a migration where plug-ins must be bypassed. Note: a cloud flow's trigger details live inside its definition and aren't shown here — open the flow in make.powerapps for that.",
  "nav.envvars": "Env Variables",
  "nav.envvars.desc": "Defaults, overrides, missing values",
  "envvars.subtitle": "Environment variables: the definition's default, the per-environment override, and — highlighted first — the ones with no value at all, the classic post-deployment trap.",
  "help.envvars_title": "Environment Variables",
  "help.envvars_body": "Every environment variable of the org: the definition's DEFAULT value and the per-environment OVERRIDE (the environmentvariablevalue row), with type badges (String, Number, Boolean, JSON, Data source, Secret) and managed/unmanaged. The headline filter is ⚠ NO VALUE: a variable with neither override nor default reads as an empty string in flows and plug-ins — they fail three screens later and nobody thinks of checking here; that's the classic post-deployment trap, so Colvio counts them in a banner. Edit or create the override (typed editor: yes/no for Booleans — the documented convention —, JSON validated before save, numbers checked), or CLEAR it to fall back to the default; production asks for confirmation and the server still enforces customizer rights. Overriding a MANAGED definition is normal — that's precisely how per-environment values are meant to be set (your override lives in the unmanaged layer). SECRET variables hold a Key Vault REFERENCE path, never the secret itself — Colvio shows and edits the reference only. CSV/Excel export of the whole inventory (default, current, effective source per variable).",
  "nav.apps": "Apps",
  "nav.apps.desc": "Model-driven app inventory",
  "apps.subtitle": "What each model-driven app actually exposes — tables, forms, views, modern commands — including the include-all forms/views status the maker portal never shows.",
  "help.apps_title": "App inventory",
  "help.apps_body": "The full component inventory of every model-driven app in the org, read straight from the runtime tables (appmodule, appmodulecomponent, systemform, savedquery, appaction). For each app you see its tables, and per table its forms and views with an EXPLICIT badge (hand-picked in the app designer) or IMPLICIT (surfaced automatically). The key insight: when a maker leaves 'All forms' / 'All views' checked, Dataverse does NOT create component rows and stores NO flag saying so — Colvio infers it from the absence of explicit form (or view) components for a registered table, independently for forms and views. ALL FORMS means every form of that table, current AND FUTURE, will appear in this app — a fact you cannot see anywhere in the maker portal. Modern command-bar buttons (appaction) are classified by their three scopes: app-specific, entity-global (surfaces in every app exposing that table), or table-generic templates (surface everywhere). The reverse search answers the other direction: type a form/view/button name and see WHICH apps expose it — the impact map you want before editing a shared form. The optional Analyze button walks the dependency graph to list the attributes and option sets an app's forms/views actually drag in (truncated at 200k edges on very large orgs — the banner says so). Classic ribbon customizations (RibbonDiffXml) are not appactions and are out of scope. Everything exports to CSV/Excel. Read-only; apps with blank display names (internal placeholders) are hidden. VIEW INSPECTOR: click any view to see its FILTERS decoded to plain language (field, operator, value, AND/OR groups, linked-table joins — an inner join hides rows without a match) and its COLUMNS — the answer to \"why doesn't my record show in that list?\" (usual culprit: the statecode = 0 filter hiding inactive rows). The ⊞ subgrids button on a form lists its child grids: which view each renders, through which relationship, and whether the view picker lets users switch views. Open in Explorer loads the view's FetchXML in the Explorer so you can add your parent's filter and test exactly why a row matches or not. Subgrids pointing at a personal view (userquery) can't be inspected — the inspector says so.",
